      "text": "To solve for the number of possible values of \\( k \\) for which the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - 63x + k = 0 \\) has two prime roots, let's start by examining its discriminant. The discriminant of a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) is given by \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\) and determines the nature of its roots. For our equation \\( x^2 - 63x + k = 0 \\), the discriminant is:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = (-63)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot k = 3969 - 4k\n\\]\n\nFor a quadratic equation with the roots \\( p \\) and \\( q \\), the equation has the form:\n\n\\[\n(x - p)(x - q) = x^2 - (p+q)x + pq\n\\]\n\nSince \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are both prime numbers and their sum, \\( p + q \\), equals 63, consider the sums and products:\n\n1. We know \\( p \\times q = k \\), and both \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are prime numbers less than 65 (since their sum is 63).\n\n2. Check pairs of prime numbers that sum to 63:\n   - 2 + 61 = 63\n\nGiven these results:\n- \\( (p, q) = (2, 61) \\) produces \\( x^2 - 63x + 122 = 0 \\).\n- The discriminant evaluates to:\n  \\[\n  \\Delta = 3969 - 4k\n  \\]\n  Setting \\( k = 122 \\):\n  \\[\n  \\Delta = 3969 - 4 \\times 122 = 3969 - 488 = 3481\n  \\]\n  Since \\( \\Delta  = 3481 \\) is a positive integer (\\( \\sqrt{3481} = 59 \\)), the roots \\( 2 \\) and \\( 61 \\) are indeed prime numbers and thus valid roots for the quadratic equation.\n\n- There are no other pairs of prime numbers that sum to 63.\n  \nTherefore, there is only one possible value of \\( k \\):\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
